hifiberry-configurator (2.18.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * New memory report at GET /api/v1/memory, and the data behind it. The
    System Information page could say how much RAM the machine has and nothing
    about who is using it, so "should I turn this off to save memory?" had no
    answer short of an SSH session and ps. The report walks /proc, attributes
    each process to its systemd unit via the leaf of its cgroup path, groups
    units into features described by drop-in JSON in
    /usr/share/hifiberry/features.d, and reports what stopping each feature
    would return.
  * It leads on PSS rather than RES. RES counts every shared page in full for
    each process using it, so on a device where config-server, sigmatcpserver
    and roomeq-server share a Python interpreter, summing RES across features
    yields far more than the machine has and overstates what stopping any one
    of them frees. PSS divides shared pages among their users and sums
    correctly.
  * Reclaimable memory is a range, not a figure. Pages shared between a
    feature's own processes are invisible to the floor, and pages it shares
    with the rest of the system are not freed by stopping it, so no exact
    answer exists. Swap is reported separately rather than added in: stopping
    a service returns its RAM and its swap, but they are different resources
    and adding them misranks a feature holding 1.6 MB of RAM and 27 MB of swap
    above one holding 30 MB of RAM.
  * Features carry a disposition saying what an owner can actually do:
    required, disable, uninstall, reconfigure, or none for a unit no
    descriptor claims. The kiosk browser has no unit of its own -- cage and cog
    run inside getty@tty1.service, which dpkg resolves to the systemd package
    -- so a descriptor may also claim a named process and its descendants.
  * GET /memory is tier ok in the auth policy, like every other read the
    System Information page makes. Without that entry it fell to the risky
    default and returned 401 on a device with no password set, so the card
    read "not available" while every card beside it worked.

 -- HiFiBerry <support@hifiberry.com>  Mi., 09 Sep. 2026 10:01:52 +0000

